e not really thinking about work  either you're not fully present for work  but companies that tell workers to  disconnect face a number of risks  belkin's research argues that  disconnection policies are not  implemented correctly they could  actually end up having negative effects  on mental health yes workers have to  have a right to disconnect however there  are certain jobs  that  prohibiting people to check email  actually might increase anxiety right  because you would be thinking what's  going on what if there is an emergency  or something else is happening  in europe where portugal's parliament  recently announced labor laws to give  workers the right not to be contacted  after hours companies have had mixed  reactions while some have complained  that such rules can hurt flexibility  others have embraced them  this is the spanish branch of global  insurer axa which introduced  disconnection policies in the collective  bargaining agreement as the right to  disconnect became law in france and  spain in 2017 and 2018.  the spanish law calls on employers to  set up internal policies that define how  employees exercise their right to  disconnect  but how does that work in practice we  had room for improvement  so that's why we took the decision to  promote it even more to to improve some  behaviors to improve some habits that  are present in the spanish culture maybe  in the past we tend to have meetings  later in the evening  we visited nuria an employee who is  mostly working from home  her job is to provide training to  managers and workers and she is required  by company policy to finish her meetings  by 6 pm  at the end of her working day nuria uses  an app to log how many hours she's  worked  so so whenever i finish my working day  what i usually do is  get in my phone and log into an app  called tempo mobile press the salida  which is  exit and i just go out to enjoy my my  day after that she says she uses her  time to catch up with friends like her  colleague alberto  according to the company's internal  surveys employee loyalty and  satisfaction at axa has improved  significantly digital disconnections  produce  more efficiency productivity  and good relationships it's hard to  determine exactly how these policies  have influenced business performance  overall axa's parrot company had a  challenging year in 2020 with shrinking  sales during the pandemic  legislation that requires companies to  refrain from contacting employees after  hours has not been enacted in the u.s  in the united states the culture is so  much work oriented i think it would be  very tough battle to pass any loss in  the united states like that  state lawmakers in new york city  proposed a right to disconnect bill in  2018 however it was opposed strongly by  business leaders during a public hearing  in 2019. we do not support the right to  disconnect bill due to concerns that  this would negatively impact our member  businesses particularly those that are  small businesses unwise to use new york  city as a testing ground for such a  novel approach  some u.s businesses have considered  introducing their own rules to help  workers to unplug loom a tech startup  developing a video communication  platform has more than 170 employees  people are working at women all around  the world its co-founder says to help  employees in different time zones not to  work after hours the startup created a  program that can disconnect people from  group chats in the communication  platform slack it's a really simple  thing it just automates the it automates  the flow of kicking out of channels  there's no hard data yet but looms says  it is evaluating the impact of the  policies which are meant to help detract  and retain talent during a period of  economic growth employees in tech have  more optionality than they've ever had  before keeping your employees happy and  engaged in this hiring market in  technology where people can work  anywhere and from anywhere a